A||||3||Patrick's Dad||ECM Connectors...Help!||||5||
Z||000000||Patrick's Dad||09-23-2001||06:21 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroECMConnectors.jpg"> <P>How do you disconnect these things? My supposition, without having broken either of them, is that they are attached, then filled with goo to keep them together for thousands of miles. Therefore, just seperate them, and deal with new goo later, when reconnecting.<P>Am I right, or way off base? If off base, then all my base are belong to you....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||09-23-2001||07:09 PM||||Those are not disconnects, but rather very fancy grommets to seal the wires where they pass through the firewall.  The one on the drivers side is for the computer harness.  It has a "C" shaped plastic retainer on the inside of the car.  Slide the retainer off and you will see a tab on the side facing the center of the car.  Carefully push this tab in (it's part of the plastic grommet piece) and the whole assembly can be removed from the engine compartment side.  The harness on the passenger side is the main wire harness feed through and can be removed in the same fashion (the "C" shaped retainer and tab are on the engine compartment side and the harness feeds into the interior).<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Rodrv6 (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick's Dad||09-23-2001||07:56 PM||||So these simply pass thru, and the wiring harnesses have to be disconnected at the ECM and other locations?<P>BTW, thanks for response.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||09-23-2001||10:07 PM||||That's right.  The easiest way I've found to do it is to disconnect the 2 connectors at the ECM box, the 2 connectors near the bottom of the ECM that tie the injection harness to the main car harness, and unbolt the data link connector, then just feed everything back into the engine compartment and lay it on the engine.  You'll find a few more things in the engine compartment that tie into the harness and need to be disconnected.  They are the fuel pump relay, A/C relay, and maybe a few others depending on just which engine and year you have.  As a side note, if you're are doing an engine removal you can unplug the main engine harness from the car harness back by the battery.  This large plug has the engine harness and the rear body harness.  After it's unplugged,(by backing out the 1/4 inch bolt in the center of it) you can seperate the 2 halves of the plug and set the engine side on the engine.  This way you can drop the engine without unhooking any of the connecters on the engine.  Short and Simple  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Rodrv6 (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
